WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.02
This is journey ten of Hewn, the software factory: queue further directions.

00:00:06.02 --> 00:00:09.47
An Owner doesn't stop having ideas while a direction runs.

00:00:09.47 --> 00:00:13.10
Hewn should take the next one straight away, hold it in line,

00:00:13.10 --> 00:00:18.04
and start it by itself when its turn comes, without letting any one Owner flood it.

00:00:18.04 --> 00:00:22.91
This video walks through how it was built: the queue, starting the next direction,

00:00:22.91 --> 00:00:25.65
the submission limit, the page, and the tests.

00:00:26.80 --> 00:00:31.51
An Owner submits further directions on a project while one is running.

00:00:31.51 --> 00:00:36.28
They queue, first in, first out, with one active direction per project,

00:00:36.28 --> 00:00:38.90
and different projects run in parallel.

00:00:38.90 --> 00:00:44.42
An Owner who submits more than ten directions in a minute gets a 429 for the extra

00:00:44.42 --> 00:00:47.11
ones, saying when they can submit again.

00:00:48.26 --> 00:00:51.42
Most of the queue has been there since journey two.

00:00:51.42 --> 00:00:55.57
A project's active direction is the one running, paused or blocked,

00:00:55.57 --> 00:00:57.43
or else its oldest queued one.

00:00:57.43 --> 00:01:02.58
Adding a feature direction checks for an active direction first: when there is one,

00:01:02.58 --> 00:01:07.36
the new direction stays queued behind it, and the handler only starts it when

00:01:07.36 --> 00:01:08.78
nothing else is active.

00:01:09.93 --> 00:01:15.09
When a direction completes, the orchestrator looks for the oldest queued direction

00:01:15.09 --> 00:01:18.24
other than the one that just ended, and starts it,

00:01:18.24 --> 00:01:20.51
instead of setting the project Idle.

00:01:20.51 --> 00:01:24.73
Cancelling a direction has taken the same path since journey seven.

00:01:24.73 --> 00:01:27.37
Each project has its own active direction,

00:01:27.37 --> 00:01:30.45
so a queue on one project never holds up another.

00:01:31.60 --> 00:01:34.19
What's new in this journey is the limit.

00:01:34.19 --> 00:01:38.90
A pipeline behavior runs ahead of validation for the two submit commands.

00:01:38.90 --> 00:01:44.00
Holding a lock for this Owner, it counts the directions they stored in the last

00:01:44.00 --> 00:01:46.39
sixty seconds, by the injected clock.

00:01:46.39 --> 00:01:51.61
At the limit, it works out when the oldest of them leaves the minute, and throws.

00:01:51.96 --> 00:01:56.78
The API turns that into a 429 with the code rate limited, the limit,

00:01:56.78 --> 00:02:00.04
the seconds to wait, and a Retry After header.

00:02:00.04 --> 00:02:05.14
Counting stored directions, instead of using ASP.NET's own rate limiter,

00:02:05.14 --> 00:02:11.02
means a restart doesn't forget the count, refused and invalid requests don't use up

00:02:11.02 --> 00:02:14.21
the minute, and the tests can move the clock.

00:02:14.21 --> 00:02:16.48
ADR fifteen records that choice.

00:02:17.63 --> 00:02:22.77
On New direction, the API library turns the 429 into a rate limited problem,

00:02:22.77 --> 00:02:28.04
and the page shows the warning callout from the mock: too many directions in a

00:02:28.04 --> 00:02:32.71
minute, how many were submitted, and when the Owner can submit again.

00:02:32.71 --> 00:02:38.12
Nothing they typed is lost, and Start direction stays disabled until the wait is

00:02:38.12 --> 00:02:38.46
over.

00:02:38.46 --> 00:02:40.97
It works in dark mode and on a phone.

00:02:42.12 --> 00:02:44.76
Each slice began with its tests failing.

00:02:44.76 --> 00:02:50.26
The backend gained four tests against the real API: the eleventh submission refused

00:02:50.26 --> 00:02:55.43
with the right wait and nothing created, the minute sliding on the test clock,

00:02:55.43 --> 00:03:00.53
refused and invalid requests not counting with each Owner limited separately,

00:03:00.53 --> 00:03:03.11
and the setting that changes the limit.

00:03:03.11 --> 00:03:08.01
The frontend gained four unit tests for the 429 and the callout's wording.

00:03:08.36 --> 00:03:12.50
All green, the backend has one hundred and ninety eight tests,

00:03:12.50 --> 00:03:17.51
the worker one hundred and forty, and the frontend ninety seven unit tests.

00:03:17.51 --> 00:03:20.05
End to end, there are three new tests.

00:03:20.05 --> 00:03:25.46
One submits two feature directions on a running project and watches each start by

00:03:25.46 --> 00:03:28.67
itself, first in, first out, with a real worker.

00:03:28.67 --> 00:03:33.48
Another fills a second Owner's minute and checks the rate limited state,

00:03:33.48 --> 00:03:36.95
and a visual test compares that state with its mock.

00:03:36.95 --> 00:03:39.89
There are fifty six end to end tests in all.

00:03:41.04 --> 00:03:44.46
Here is Ledgerline, with its product direction running.

00:03:44.46 --> 00:03:49.36
The Owner opens New direction, chooses Feature for a product, picks Ledgerline,

00:03:49.36 --> 00:03:52.53
and adds a CSV export to the reconciliation report.

00:03:52.53 --> 00:03:57.38
Back on the project page, a callout says one direction is queued and names it.

00:03:57.38 --> 00:04:02.22
Then a second feature, emailing the reviewer a summary when a month is closed.

00:04:02.22 --> 00:04:07.07
Now two directions are queued, and the callout names the one that starts next.

00:04:07.42 --> 00:04:10.53
The queue reads the same in dark mode and on a phone.

00:04:10.88 --> 00:04:13.93
The product direction reaches its last stages.

00:04:13.93 --> 00:04:18.97
When it is Complete, the CSV export starts by itself as a feature direction,

00:04:18.97 --> 00:04:24.01
with its skipped stages struck through, and the callout counts the one still

00:04:24.01 --> 00:04:24.54
waiting.

00:04:24.54 --> 00:04:26.13
Nobody pressed anything.

00:04:26.48 --> 00:04:31.37
And here is a second Owner who has already submitted ten directions in the last

00:04:31.37 --> 00:04:31.80
minute.

00:04:31.80 --> 00:04:36.07
The eleventh is refused: the callout says when they can submit again,

00:04:36.07 --> 00:04:38.60
and the form keeps everything they typed.

00:04:39.75 --> 00:04:41.31
Journey ten is complete.

00:04:41.31 --> 00:04:46.42
Further directions queue first in, first out, one active direction per project,

00:04:46.42 --> 00:04:51.22
and each starts by itself when the one before it is Complete or Cancelled.

00:04:51.22 --> 00:04:56.66
An Owner can submit ten directions a minute; the extra ones get a 429 saying when to

00:04:56.66 --> 00:04:59.64
try again, and the page keeps what they typed.

00:04:59.64 --> 00:05:03.08
Reordering or removing queued directions comes later.

00:05:03.08 --> 00:05:05.80
Next comes journey eleven: find a project.
